#### Computer Vision\nL85: \nL86: #### SAM 3.1: Faster and More Accessible Real-Time Video Detection and Tracking With Multiplexing and Global Reasoning\nL87: \nL88: March 27, 2026\nL89: \nL90: cite23†Learn More L91: \nL92: Blog\nL93: \nL94: March 11, 2026\nL95: #### Four MTIA Chips in Two Years: Scaling AI Experiences for Billions\nL96: \nL97: Serving a wide range of AI models on a global scale, while maintaining the lowest possible costs, is one of the most demanding infrastructure challenges in the industry.\nL98: \nL99: March 11, 2026\nL100: \nL101: cite24†Learn More L102: \nL103: Blog\nL104: \nL105: December 18, 2025\nL106: \nL107: #### Computer Vision\nL108: #### How DINO and SAM are Helping Modernize Essential Medical Triage Practices\nL109: \nL110: By leveraging advanced AI models, teams at the University of Pennsylvania are aiming to bring cutting-edge automation to emergency response.\nL111: \nL112: December 18, 2025\nL113: \nL114: cite25†Learn More L115: \nL116: Blog\nL117: \nL118: February 09, 2026\nL119: \nL120: #### Computer Vision\nL121: \nL122: #### Open Source\nL123: #### Reducing Government Costs and Increasing Access to Greenspaces in the United Kingdom with DINO\nL124: \nL125: Meta's DINOv2 model is enhancing reforestation efforts around the world. Learn how the UK government is using DINO to help reduce costs and increase access to greenspaces.\nL126: \nL127: February 09, 2026\nL128: \nL129: cite26†Learn More L130: \nL131: Blog\nL132: \nL133: February 20, 2025\nL134: \nL135: #### Open Source\nL136: #### How Orakl Oncology is using DINOv2 to accelerate cancer treatment discovery\nL137: \nL138: Orakl Oncology aims to accelerate cancer research and drug development by combining experimental, lab-based insights and machine learning.\nL139: \nL140: February 20, 2025\nL141: \nL142: cite27†Learn More L143: \nL144: Blog\nL145: \nL146: December 18, 2025\nL147: \nL148: #### Computer Vision\nL149: #### The Universities Space Research Association Applies Segment Anything Model for Responding to Flood Emergencies\nL150: \nL151: The Universities Space Research Association and Meta are collaborating to help support water observing systems set up by the U.S. Geological Survey.\nL152: \nL153: December 18, 2025\nL154: \nL155: cite28†Learn More L156: \nL157: Blog\nL158: \nL159: December 16, 2025\nL160: \nL161: #### Computer Vision\nL162: #### Introducing SAM Audio: The First Unified Multimodal Model for Audio Separation\nL163: \nL164: SAM Audio transforms audio processing by making it easy to isolate any sound from complex audio mixtures using natural, multimodal prompts — whether through text, visual cues, or marking time segments.\nL165: \nL166: December 16, 2025\nL167: \nL168: cite29†Learn More L169: \nL170: Blog\nL171: \nL172: November 19, 2025\nL173: \nL174: #### Computer Vision\nL175: #### Introducing SAM 3D: Powerful 3D Reconstruction for Physical World Images\nL176: \nL177: This release introduces two new state-of-the-art models: SAM 3D Objects for object and scene reconstruction, and SAM 3D Body for human body and shape estimation.\nL178: \nL179: November 19, 2025\nL180: \nL181: cite30†Learn More L182: \nL183: Blog\nL184: \nL185: November 24, 2025\nL186: \nL187: #### Computer Vision\nL188: #### How Conservation X Labs Is Using Segment Anything Model 3 for Endangered Wildlife Monitoring\nL189: \nL190: Start-ups like Conservation X Labs are using Meta’s Segment Anything Models to bolster on-the-ground conservation expertise.\nL191: \nL192: November 24, 2025\nL193: \nL194: cite31†Learn More L195: \nL196: Blog\nL197: \nL198: November 21, 2025\nL199: \nL200: #### Open Source\nL201: #### ExecuTorch Adoption in Reality Labs: Powering On-Device AI Across Meta Devices\nL202: \nL203: ExecuTorch, Meta’s open source, lightweight, and efficient inference engine, has been instrumental in enabling on-device AI capabilities across Meta’s family of apps.\nL204: \nL205: November 21, 2025\nL206: \nL207: cit